# Article: Pack (six)

Roles: specifier, coder, cleaner, architect, hardener, qa.

Chain: specifier -> coder -> cleaner -> architect -> hardener -> qa.
A passing qa broadcasts completion to every other role; a qa finding
routes back to specifier and the chain runs again.

- specifier turns a request into an accepted specification, then sends a
  git_handoff to coder.
- coder implements the specified slice, then forwards to cleaner.
- cleaner cleans the implementation, then forwards to architect.
- architect reviews structure and boundaries, then forwards to hardener.
- hardener produces quality evidence (coverage, mutation, complexity,
  duplication) and fixes what fails the bars, then forwards to qa.
- qa runs the acceptance and unit suites and verifies the slice against
  its specification. A substantive finding — delivered behavior that
  contradicts the specification, or specified behavior with no test —
  goes back to specifier as a git_handoff carrying the qa commit; that
  handoff continues the chain, and specifier slices the finding into new
  work. Only a passing verification ends the slice: qa then sends the
  completion git_handoff to specifier,coder,cleaner,architect,hardener,
  and that broadcast is TERMINAL — each recipient merges it and stops.
- Routing back is bounded: at most two findings per task name. When a
  third verification of the same task fails, qa stops and puts the
  situation to the user instead of sending the slice round again. Six
  roles per iteration, one of them a mutation pass, is too expensive to
  loop unattended, and a task that fails verification three times is a
  specification problem rather than a fix-it-again problem.
